somewhere out there beyond the moon but  not quite past Jupiter is roughly 5  meter-wide space rock that may one day  get a more permanent home near Earth  within the next decade or so NASA wants  to capture that asteroid and haul it to  the moon relocating the rock is part of  the asteroid redirect mission which  could help humans get ready for an  eventual trip to Mars or so nASA says  politics aside the agency's two plans to  nab a space rock and bring it closer to  the moon are pretty fantastic in the  first scenario a spacecraft will blast  off from Earth and sail through the  solar system toward the asteroid when  positioned just right the spacecraft  will unfurl a bag that when inflated  will stretch about as tall as a  three-story building and hold an  asteroid weighing up to a thousand tons  the bag will then be slipped around the  rock and cinched shut like a garbage  sack the spacecraft will fire up its  engines and slowly gently begin nudging  the rock toward its new orbit around the  moon a journey that could take years  NASA's second proposed plan to nab a  space rock involves a robot with some  combination of arms legs and possibly  thousands of little hooks this robot  will be designed to snatch a golf cart  size Boulder off the target asteroid and  shuttle it to the moon but the hunk of  rock won't go from deep space to the  moon without leaving its parent asteroid  completely unaffected if nasa chooses  this concept the agency wants to tug on  the asteroid with a gravity tractor the  tugging would use the tiny attraction  between the boulder snatching spacecraft  and the parent asteroid the idea would  test a technique that could one day help  nudge huge space rocks off course and  keep them from hitting Earth ultimately  NASA wants to bring an asteroid to the  moon so that humans can explore it the  space agency argues that either scenario  will help get people closer to the red  planet without the mission the u.s. may  not have much of a future in human space  exploration of course only time will  tell if the moon will one day get a  rocky satellite of its own
 

